    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "That makes sense. Could you go a bit deeper into the CNS (central nervous system) side of things? Like, why does a deload specifically help the nervous system recover compared to just taking a few extra days of rest between heavy sessions?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "When an individual is subjected to a prolonged overload, the CNS is under constant stress, leading to increased neural excitability and probability of muscle fibersailing into spastic states. During a deload, reducing volume reduces this neural drive, allowing the CNS to regenerate and reorganize its motor patterns, which are critical for proper muscle fiber activation and firing. Removing the stress from the CNS promotes re-synchronization and fine-tuning, rather than just compensating for increased fatigue (which occurs during more traditional recovery rest).",
      "source": "target",
